---
import ChronicleLayout from '../../layouts/ChronicleLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleHeader.astro';
import AnchorNav from '../../components/chronicle/AnchorNav.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/chronicle/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/chronicle/Figure.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/chronicle/SectionHeading.astro';
import AtAGlance from '../../components/chronicle/AtAGlance.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/chronicle/FurtherReading.astro';
import type { ChronicleMeta } from '../../components/chronicle/types';
import { SIZES_MODERN_WIDE } from '../../lib/responsive-image-config.mjs';

const meta: ChronicleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Things to Do in Buenos Aires: A First-Hand Guide',
  headline: 'Best Things to Do in Buenos Aires',
  description:
    'A first-hand guide to the best things to do in Buenos Aires, from Plaza de Mayo and Teatro Colón to Recoleta, San Telmo, La Boca, Puerto Madero and Palermo.',
  path: '/best-things-to-do-in-buenos-aires/',
  ogImage: '/images/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/buenos-aires-city-centre-1200w.jpg',
  photography: true,
};

const IMG = '/images/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary';
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Argentina', 'Buenos Aires', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · Ten Experiences"
    title="Best Things to Do in Buenos Aires"
    standfirst="Buenos Aires was not one monumental centre. It was a sequence of different city worlds: civic squares, cultural interiors, markets, museums, waterfront architecture, football streets and Palermo parks. These are the experiences from my September–October 2026 stay that deserve priority on a first visit."
    stats={[
      { num: '10', label: 'Experiences' },
      { num: '5', label: 'Nights' },
      { num: '6', label: 'City areas' },
      { num: '1', label: 'First visit' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Buenos Aires did not reveal itself through one landmark. It arrived in
          sections: the civic scale of Plaza de Mayo, the Obelisk and Teatro Colón;
          the quieter concentration of books, museums and Recoleta; then markets,
          waterfront buildings, football architecture and the green edge of Palermo.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is the broad discovery guide I would have wanted before the trip. It
          is not another five-day itinerary and it is not a neighbourhood comparison.
          The question here is simpler: what is worth choosing when time is limited?
          The recommendations come from places recorded during my stay from 29
          September to 4 October 2026, with the judgement kept close to what the
          route and photographs can actually support.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have left out tango, a completed Palermo run, football-match attendance,
          restaurant rankings and current visitor logistics. La Bombonera appears as
          a stadium stop, not a match-day experience. Colonia del Sacramento is kept
          separate as an optional day trip rather than folded into Buenos Aires.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-city-centre.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-city-centre-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-city-centre-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-city-centre-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-city-centre-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-city-centre.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A broad Buenos Aires civic-centre sequence; the exact landmark remains deliberately unnamed."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Buenos Aires begins as a civic sequence, but the city quickly opens into other worlds."
      priority
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="how-to-use" rule={false}>How to use this list</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The order is a way of moving through the city, not a claim that one landmark
        is objectively better than another. The first four establish the central
        cultural spine. Recoleta, San Telmo, Puerto Madero, La Boca and Palermo then
        widen the picture. The museum recommendation gathers several confirmed stops
        rather than turning every recorded institution into a thin list entry.
      </p>
      <p>
        Route details below describe my recorded visit. They are not current opening
        hours, queue estimates, fares or guaranteed travel times. For the actual
        chronology, use the <a href="/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/">five-day Buenos Aires itinerary</a>;
        for a geographic comparison, use the <a href="/buenos-aires-neighbourhoods/">Buenos Aires neighbourhoods guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AnchorNav
      links={[
        { id: 'historic-centre', label: 'Historic centre' },
        { id: 'obelisk', label: 'Obelisk' },
        { id: 'teatro-colon', label: 'Teatro Colón' },
        { id: 'el-ateneo', label: 'El Ateneo' },
        { id: 'recoleta', label: 'Recoleta' },
        { id: 'san-telmo', label: 'San Telmo' },
        { id: 'puerto-madero', label: 'Puerto Madero' },
        { id: 'la-boca', label: 'La Boca' },
        { id: 'palermo', label: 'Palermo parks' },
        { id: 'museums', label: 'Museums' },
        { id: 'priorities', label: 'Priorities' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <AtAGlance
    id="glance"
    title="The Ten Experiences at a Glance"
    meta={[
      {
        label: 'Start here',
        value: 'Plaza de Mayo, the Obelisk, Teatro Colón and El Ateneo form the strongest central introduction to the city.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Add for contrast',
        value: 'Recoleta, San Telmo, Puerto Madero, La Boca and Palermo show how quickly the city changes register.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Keep separate',
        value: 'Colonia is an optional day-trip spoke. It is not one of the core things to do in Buenos Aires.',
      },
    ]}
    daysHeading="Choose by interest"
    days={[
      { num: 'Civic', text: 'Plaza de Mayo · Casa Rosada area · the Obelisk · central architecture' },
      { num: 'Culture', text: 'Teatro Colón · El Ateneo · museums · Recoleta’s cultural route' },
      { num: 'Street', text: 'San Telmo markets · La Boca route · Palermo’s parks and streets' },
      { num: 'Water', text: 'Puerto Madero · Silos · Fortabat and the contemporary waterfront route' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter id="historic-centre" number="I." name="Explore Plaza de Mayo and the historic centre" epithet="The civic route that gave the first day its shape" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Plaza de Mayo was the clearest starting point. On 29 September, the recorded
        arrival route moved through Buenos Aires Metropolitan Cathedral, the Casa
        Rosada area, the Block of the Lights Historical-Cultural Complex and Plaza de
        Mayo before continuing towards Plaza del Congreso and the National Museum of
        Fine Arts.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would choose this route first because it introduces Buenos Aires through
        public space and civic architecture rather than through a single attraction.
        The specialist <a href="/plaza-de-mayo-buenos-aires/">Plaza de Mayo and historic Buenos Aires guide</a>
        keeps the same limits visible: the stops are confirmed, but the archive does
        not preserve building entry, a measured walking route or current visitor details.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-congreso-sequence.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-congreso-sequence-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-congreso-sequence-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-congreso-sequence-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-congreso-sequence.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Plaza del Congreso and central civic architecture in Buenos Aires."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="The historic-centre route continued from Plaza de Mayo towards Congress."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="obelisk" number="II." name="See the Obelisk and central Buenos Aires" epithet="A landmark within a wider sequence of civic and cultural buildings" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Obelisk was recorded at the beginning of the 30 September route, before
        Palacio San Martín, the Museo del Agua y de la Historia Sanitaria and Teatro
        Colón. It deserves a stop, but not an entire theory of the city. Its value in
        this trip was as one unmistakable point in a day that kept moving through
        central architecture, museums and interiors.
      </p>
      <p>
        Palacio Barolo, Galerías Pacífico, Palacio Libertad and Basílica del Santísimo
        Sacramento also belong to the wider central sequence. I keep them as supporting
        stops rather than separate “best things” entries: the hub should help choose a
        route, not turn every recorded building into a ranking.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-obelisco.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-obelisco-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-obelisco-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-obelisco-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-obelisco.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The Obelisk in central Buenos Aires."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="The Obelisk: a fixed landmark inside a much larger central-city route."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="teatro-colon" number="III." name="Make time for Teatro Colón" epithet="One of the strongest cultural anchors in the central route" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Teatro Colón belongs near the top of the list because it was part of the
        densest cultural day, alongside the Obelisk, Palacio San Martín, El Ateneo and
        Palacio Barolo. The surviving route and image family establish a Teatro Colón
        sequence, but they do not let me upgrade that into a confirmed interior tour,
        performance or detailed access account.
      </p>
      <p>
        That distinction does not make the stop weak. It makes the recommendation
        precise: include Teatro Colón in a central cultural route, then verify current
        access separately if you want a tour or performance. This page is about the
        experience that the trip actually records, not a timetable reconstructed from
        general knowledge.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-teatro-colon-sequence.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-teatro-colon-sequence-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-teatro-colon-sequence-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-teatro-colon-sequence-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-teatro-colon-sequence.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Teatro Colón sequence in Buenos Aires; exterior or interior is not specified."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="Teatro Colón sequence; the archive does not resolve exterior versus interior."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="el-ateneo" number="IV." name="See El Ateneo Grand Splendid" epithet="A bookstore stop that changes the scale of the central route" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        El Ateneo followed Teatro Colón in the 30 September record. It is one of the
        places I would retain even on a compressed first visit because it changes the
        kind of space on offer: after civic buildings and a theatre, the route turns
        towards books and a more intimate interior sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        The photograph supports the El Ateneo identification, but the evidence does
        not preserve a detailed review of the shop, a purchase, a time spent inside or
        a judgement about its collection. The useful recommendation is simply to put
        it beside Teatro Colón and Palacio Barolo in the central cultural route.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-el-ateneo-sequence.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-el-ateneo-sequence-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-el-ateneo-sequence-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-el-ateneo-sequence-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-el-ateneo-sequence.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="El Ateneo sequence in Buenos Aires; the image is used without adding a detailed interior review."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="El Ateneo in the recorded central-city cultural sequence."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="recoleta" number="V." name="Follow Recoleta’s cultural route" epithet="Floralis, museums and a cemetery stop without pretending to know more than the record" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Recoleta is strongest as a cultural route rather than as a single attraction.
        The 30 September sequence includes Recoleta Cemetery, Floralis Genérica, the
        Museo Nacional de Arte Decorativo and MALBA after the central-city stops.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have not turned the cemetery into an exploration article. The itinerary
        confirms the stop, but it does not preserve a detailed account of which parts
        were visited. Floralis Genérica is the clearest photographic anchor. Around it,
        the museums make Recoleta worth choosing when you want a day that moves between
        monumental space and art rather than only between landmarks.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Floralis Genérica in the Recoleta and Palermo route."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Floralis Genérica: the clearest landmark in the recorded Recoleta–Palermo route."
    />

    <Chapter id="san-telmo" number="VI." name="Choose San Telmo’s market-and-art route" epithet="A day that moved between Mercado San Telmo, Museo Moderno and the fair" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        San Telmo added a different kind of movement. The recorded routes include
        Mercado San Telmo, Museo Moderno and, on the final full day, the San Telmo Fair.
        Those stops are enough to recommend the area as a market-and-art experience;
        they are not enough to repeat the usual shorthand about bohemian streets,
        tango on every corner or an antiques culture I did not document.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would pair San Telmo with the waterfront route rather than make it the whole
        day by default. The existing <a href="/buenos-aires-neighbourhoods/">neighbourhood guide</a>
        compares San Telmo with the city’s other districts; this page’s narrower point
        is that the market and museum stops deserve a place in the shortlist.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-mural-sequence.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-mural-sequence-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-mural-sequence-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-mural-sequence-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-mural-sequence.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Buenos Aires mural or street-art sequence; exact location is unresolved."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A Buenos Aires mural sequence; the exact neighbourhood is not assigned from the photograph alone."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="puerto-madero" number="VII." name="Walk Puerto Madero’s waterfront and cultural route" epithet="Contemporary architecture and art after the market streets" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Puerto Madero supplied the sharpest change in architectural register. The
        1 October route moved from San Telmo through Silos Puerto Madero and the
        waterfront to the Amalia Lacroze de Fortabat Art Collection, with Faena Art
        Center also recorded in the wider cultural sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would choose this route when the older civic and market city needs a
        counterpoint. The evidence supports the waterfront, silos and cultural stops;
        it does not support turning Puerto Madero into a restaurant, nightlife or
        safety guide. The recommendation is for the combination of water, modern
        architecture and art spaces.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-modern-architecture.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-modern-architecture-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-modern-architecture-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-modern-architecture-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-modern-architecture.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Modern Buenos Aires architecture from the wider central and waterfront route; exact venue is unresolved."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Modern Buenos Aires architecture from the wider central and waterfront route."
    />

    <Chapter id="la-boca" number="VIII." name="Follow the La Boca route" epithet="Caminito, art, the bridge and a stadium stop—without inventing a match" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        La Boca was the opening section of the final full Buenos Aires route. The
        itinerary records Caminito, Pasaje Garibaldi, Puente Transbordador, Fundación
        Proa and Estadio Alberto J. Armando, La Bombonera. That is a distinct route and
        a strong reason to include La Boca in a first visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The football distinction matters. La Bombonera is supported as a stadium stop,
        not as a match, ticketed tour or match-day atmosphere. The 3 October route has
        no matching Buenos Aires photo-date coverage, so this section stays text-led.
        A football-themed image elsewhere in the archive would not prove that I was
        standing at La Bombonera or watching a game.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="palermo" number="IX." name="Slow down in Palermo’s parks" epithet="The green and open end of the recorded city route" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Palermo was both the recorded accommodation base and the setting for the final
        green sequence: Plaza Serrano, El Rosedal Garden, Planetario Galileo Galilei
        and Paseo de La Infanta. These places make Palermo worth choosing when the trip
        needs space after the compressed central and museum routes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The running notes attached to the Palermo parks were planning material, not a
        completed run. I have therefore kept the recommendation with the parks,
        Planetarium and route structure rather than describing a jog, distance or
        recovery. For the wider geographic comparison, the <a href="/buenos-aires-neighbourhoods/">neighbourhood guide</a>
        is the better destination.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-streetscape.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-streetscape-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-streetscape-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-streetscape-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-streetscape.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Buenos Aires streetscape from the wider city route; exact location is unresolved."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Buenos Aires streetscape from the wider city route; exact location is deliberately unresolved."
    />

    <Chapter id="museums" number="X." name="Choose the museums that fit your route" epithet="A cultural network rather than a checklist of every institution" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Buenos Aires offered enough museums and cultural buildings to make selection
        necessary. The recorded route includes the Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes,
        Museo Nacional de Arte Decorativo, MALBA, Museo del Agua y de la Historia
        Sanitaria, Museo Moderno, Faena Art Center, the Fortabat collection, Palacio
        Libertad and Galerías Pacífico.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would not present that list as a universal ranking. Instead, use it to choose
        a cultural emphasis: Bellas Artes, Decorativo and MALBA fit naturally with the
        Recoleta route; Museo Moderno and Faena fit with San Telmo and Puerto Madero;
        Fortabat, Palacio Libertad and Galerías Pacífico belong to the wider central
        and waterfront sequence. The evidence confirms the stops, not a gallery-by-
        gallery review of each collection.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-gallery-interior.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-gallery-interior-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-gallery-interior-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-gallery-interior-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-gallery-interior.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Gallery or exhibition interior in Buenos Aires; exact venue is unresolved."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A gallery or exhibition interior from the recorded cultural route; exact venue unresolved."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <SectionHeading id="priorities">What I Would Prioritise</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        <strong>With one day:</strong> I would keep the route central: Plaza de Mayo,
        the Obelisk, Teatro Colón and El Ateneo, with one museum or the Recoleta route
        if time allows. That gives the first day a civic and cultural spine without
        pretending the entire city can be covered.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With three days:</strong> keep the central route on one day, use a
        second for Recoleta and Palermo or San Telmo and Puerto Madero, then give the
        remaining day to La Boca and the parks. The choice depends on whether museums,
        markets, waterfront architecture or neighbourhood movement matter most; the
        evidence does not justify a single universal order.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With five days:</strong> use the <a href="/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/">complete five-day itinerary</a>
        for the recorded chronology, including the separate Colonia day. This page is
        the selection layer above it, not a replacement for the dates.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="surprised">What surprised me most</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The strongest impression was structural. Buenos Aires did not stay at one
        scale. A civic square led to a theatre and a bookstore; the cultural route
        opened into Recoleta’s museums, then San Telmo’s market-and-art sequence and
        Puerto Madero’s waterfront architecture. La Boca and Palermo changed the
        texture again.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why I would resist a list made entirely from famous names. The useful
        question is not whether one attraction is the city’s “best”, but which change
        of register you want next. The recommendations above are the places that made
        those changes visible in the route I actually recorded.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-closing-streetscape.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-closing-streetscape-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-closing-streetscape-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-closing-streetscape-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-closing-streetscape.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Buenos Aires streetscape from the wider route; exact location is unresolved."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The route ended not with one final monument, but with another change of city texture."
    />

    <SectionHeading id="colonia">If you have an extra day: Colonia del Sacramento</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Colonia del Sacramento was recorded as a separate 2 October day trip from
        Buenos Aires. I keep it outside the core list because it is in Uruguay, and
        because the transport operator, crossing details, completed sightseeing and
        photo coverage remain unresolved in the source pack.
      </p>
      <p>
        The dedicated <a href="/colonia-del-sacramento-day-trip/">Colonia del Sacramento day-trip account</a>
        keeps that route record separate. It is the right place to investigate the
        extension once the remaining travel details are confirmed; it should not be
        used to inflate a list of things to do inside Buenos Aires.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="omitted">What I have left out</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        I have not added tango because no attended performance or milonga is confirmed.
        I have not described a Palermo run because the route notes are planning only.
        I have not turned La Bombonera into a match report, or any restaurant into a
        ranking, because those claims are not supported by the archive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current prices, fares, opening hours, queues, weather, safety conditions and
        transport procedure also remain outside this first-hand guide. They change, and
        they were not part of the supplied evidence. This page is strongest when it
        helps decide what deserves time without pretending to be a current operations
        manual.
      </p>
    </div>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through Buenos Aires"
      cards={[
        { href: '/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/', region: 'Argentina · Buenos Aires', title: '5 Days in Buenos Aires: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full chronology through the historic centre, Recoleta, Palermo, San Telmo, Puerto Madero, La Boca and the recorded Colonia day.', meta: ['Itinerary', 'September–October 2026'] },
        { href: '/buenos-aires-neighbourhoods/', region: 'Argentina · Buenos Aires', title: 'Buenos Aires Neighbourhoods: Exploring the City Barrio by Barrio', excerpt: 'A geographic comparison of Palermo, Recoleta, San Telmo, Puerto Madero, La Boca and the historic centre.', meta: ['Neighbourhoods', 'September–October 2026'] },
        { href: '/plaza-de-mayo-buenos-aires/', region: 'Argentina · Buenos Aires', title: 'Plaza de Mayo & Historic Buenos Aires', excerpt: 'A focused arrival-day account of the Cathedral, Casa Rosada area, Manzana de las Luces, Plaza de Mayo and Congress.', meta: ['Historic centre', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/colonia-del-sacramento-day-trip/', region: 'Uruguay · Colonia del Sacramento', title: 'Colonia del Sacramento Day Trip from Buenos Aires', excerpt: 'A separate, carefully bounded account of the recorded day beyond Buenos Aires.', meta: ['Day trip', 'October 2026'] },
        { href: '/24-days-in-south-america-itinerary/', region: 'South America · Argentina', title: '24 Days in South America', excerpt: 'The wider route from Lima to Buenos Aires, with the city as its final urban base.', meta: ['Pillar itinerary', '2026'] },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
</ChronicleLayout>
